 $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- The relation \"$⊄$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since ${2,3}⊄{2}$ but ${2}⊂{2,3}.$\n\n- The relation \"$\\ne $\" for the cardinalities of sets is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $|{2}|=|{2}|.$\n\n- The relation \"$⊇$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $N⊇{1}$ but ${1}⊉N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421405 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421405 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421405 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421405 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $R?$\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421405 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a65003f-5b78-57dd-8ad6-282231fb787b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421405 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $2=2(1)$ but $1\\ne 2(2).$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $x\\in R,$ we have $x-x=0$ is a rational number.\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $x,y\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ is a rational number, then $y-x=-(x-y)=-r$ is also a rational number.\n\n- It is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ and $y-z=q,$ where $r,q$ are rational numbers, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)=r+q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421406 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421406 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421406 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421406 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following relation defined on the set $P(A),$ where $A={1,2,3,…,10}.$ \n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,|X|=|Y|\n$$\n\n Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421406 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b086cc6-e229-5377-bb89-5a8ff879bb27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421406 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $X\\in P(A),$ $|X|\\,=\\,|X|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. For all $X,Y\\in P(A),$ if $|X|\\,=\\,|Y|,$ then $|Y|=|X|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z\\in P(A),$ if $|X|=|Y|$ and $|Y|=|Z|,$ then $|X|=|Z|.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the relation $y∣x$ defined on the set $Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n| The relation is reflexive | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- |\n| The relation is symmetric | {{field-2}} |\n| The relation is transitive | {{field-3}} |\n| The relation is an equivalence relation | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ce6b951-f687-5e96-9bbc-0957d6d3d0b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421407 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is reflexive. For all $x\\in Z,$ $x∣x.$\n\n- It's $\\text{false}$ that the relation is symmetric. For example, $1∣2$ but $2∤1.$\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is transitive. For all $x,y,z\\in Z,$ if $y∣x$ and $z∣y,$ then $z∣x.$\n\n- It's $\\text{false}$ that this is an equivalence relation since it's not symmetric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421408 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421408 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421408 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421408 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A⊃B$\n- $A=B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c8d639d-4ba9-5766-b837-cc9cfbce29a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421408 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}\\cap {1}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation II is also not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because ${1}⊅{1}.$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation since it's reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421409 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={∗,⋆,∘,⋄}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘),(⋄,⋄),(∗,⋆),(⋆,∗)}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ee391cd-217d-5ff1-a899-24ea6980b5eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421409 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘),(⋄,⋄)\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in {∗,⋆,∘,⋄},$ if  $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$  then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $(∗,⋆)\\in R$ and $(⋆,∗)\\in R,$ and also $(∗,∗)\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $(⋆,∗)\\in R$ and $(∗,⋆)\\in R,$ and also $(⋆,⋆)\\in R$$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation.$\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421410 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\beta ,\\beta ),(\\beta ,\\alpha ),(\\alpha ,\\beta )}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72d1402f-e4b5-5dff-b1dc-9142e68fa197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421410 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$