 $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is reflexive. For all $X\\in P(Q),$ $X\\,⊆\\,X.$\n\n- It's $\\text{false}$ that the relation is symmetric. For example, $N⊆Z$ but $Z⊈N.$\n\n- It's $\\text{true}$ that the relation is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z\\in P(Q),$ if $X⊆Y$ and $Y⊆Z,$ then $X⊆Z.$\n\n- It's $\\text{false}$ that this is an equivalence relation since it's not symmetric." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
